ive places to the right it's  getting larger so that's going to be to  move that one place that's one and then  we have four placeholders which are the  zeros so it's two hundred and ten  thousand now if this had been two 0.1  times 10 to the negative fifth we would  actually move the decimal point one two  three four five to the left so for  number 65 it says write in scientific  notation so here we're going backwards  here so what you want when you write a  number in scientific notation is you  just want one digit between one and nine  to the left or in front of the decimal  point and then the other significant  digits to the right of the decimal point  and then times ten to a power so here  what I have to do is I have to move this  decimal point right there so that  there's just one digit in front of the  decimal point so this is one point three  times  10 to the 1 2 3 4 this is a negative 4  because the negative when you have a  negative exponent there you move that  decimal 4 to the left and that's going  to be giving us the standard form of the  number now one thing you can remember is  that if it's a really small number see  point zero zero zero this is a really  small number this is going to be a  negative exponent if it's a really large  number it's gonna be a positive exponent  that's just one way to kind of remember  but for this one the decimal point is  right here we really want it to be right  there we want to move it one two three  four five six so there's just one digit  to the left or in front of that decimal  point so this is four point eight one  times 10 to the what do we say one two  three four five six 10 to the sixth  power okay for number 66 we're back to  the rules of exponents simplify x  squared to the fourth power so how would  you do that on what's x squared to the  fourth well when you have a power to a  power what you do is you multiply the  exponents sometimes students get  confused when you multiply you add  exponents but when you have a power to a  power you times the exponents so how  would you do this one three why cubes  the quantity squared well when you  square this what you want to do is  realize that the number also has an  exponent it's just the first power and  you distribute that power on the outside  to these exponents on the inside so this  becomes 3 squared which is 9 and this  becomes 3 times 2 which is y to the 6 so  don't forget to distribute the power to  the number as well as the variable for  number 67 it says is the graph linear or  nonlinear so when we talk about linear  we're talking about straight lines  nonlinear is just like saying it's not a  straight line it's not linear so what do  you think for this one well you can see  it's like more of a curve it's not a  straight line so we would say this one  is nonlinear for 68 it says find the  square root so you recognize this  radical here the square root symbol this  tells us what number times itself is 36  so in this case you can see 6 times 6 is  36 and then times that negative will  make this negative 6 so you work from  the inside out for 69 it says estimate  the square root so square root of 83  what do you think that would be  approximately well we know that the  square root of a hundred  is 10 because 10 times 10 is 100 we know  the square root of 81 is 9 because 9  times 9 is 81  but 83 is closer to 81 than it is to a  hundred so I would say square root of 83  is approximately 9 okay so we're just  rounding to the nearest integer for  number 70 it says find X so you can see  they give us a triangle here what do you  think how would you solve for the  measure of angle X well the thing to  remember is that triangles all the  angles add up to 180  whereas with quadrilaterals four-sided  figures angles add up to 360 so in this  case let's see we've got 88 plus 32 so  that would be how much there'd be a  hundred and 20 so that means that X has  to be 60 degrees okay let's go to number  71 it says classify the triangle 